{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01WXvv2vrkurxXiuXFp7nYx7","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 10 questions are present, the file is 599 lines. Done.\n\nHere's a summary of what was written to `/answers/02-payment-security-pci-compliance.md`:\n\n---\n\n**What's in the file (599 lines, 10 questions answered):**\n\n**Core Questions:**\n\n1. **PCI DSS scope & SAQ reduction** — Explains SAQ A vs. A-EP vs. D tiers with a comparison table, and exactly why hosted fields qualify you for SAQ A (no PAN touches your origin).\n\n2. **Stripe Elements postMessage mechanism** — Mermaid sequence diagram tracing the full lifecycle from `elements.create()` → iframe injection → user typing → SDK `postMessage` → direct iframe-to-Stripe API submission → `pm_...` token returned to your page. Covers what `onChange` events contain (metadata only, never PAN).\n\n3. **Cross-origin iframe styling** — Explains why parent CSS is blocked by same-origin policy, how Stripe's `appearance` config API serializes styles and injects them via `postMessage` into the iframe's own document, and which CSS properties are achievable vs. impossible regardless of configuration.\n\n4. **CSP configuration for Stripe** — Full production CSP header with directive-by-directive breakdown, covers the `frame-src`, `connect-src` (for `q.stripe.com` Radar pixels), and the uncomfortable `script-src https://js.stripe.com` trust decision. Flags `unsafe-inline` and missing `report-uri` as the two most common misconfigurations.\n\n5. **Tokenization lifecycle** — Mermaid sequence diagram covering browser → Stripe API (iframe-direct) → your server → Stripe API (secret key) → webhook. Table showing exactly what goes where (PAN: never to your server; `pm_...`: safe to log).\n\n6. **Client secret storage risks** — Ranked from `localStorage` (worst) to in-memory React state (correct), with XSS attack code example showing how a stored secret is exfiltrated, plus the bounded-capability analysis of what an attacker can actually *do* with a stolen client secret.\n\n7. **Clickjacking defense** — Why `X-Frame-Options: DENY` is not broken alongside Stripe iframes (the header governs who frames *you*, not who *you* frame), why CSP `frame-ancestors` supersedes XFO, and when JavaScript frame-busting is appropriate as defense-in-depth.\n\n**Deep Questions:**\n\n8. **Custom fonts in cross-origin iframes** — Explains that `@font-face` in parent CSS is inaccessible from the iframe, walks through how Stripe's `fonts` array triggers the iframe to construct its own `@font-face` rule and fetch from your CDN (requiring CORS headers), and lists what's architecturally impossible (variable font axes, per-field fonts). Proposes the practical design compromise.\n\n9. **Pen tester's client secret finding** — Structured evaluation framework: what the client secret *can and cannot* do, the five server-side controls required to bound its blast radius (auth, server-determined amount, userId metadata binding, webhook validation, short expiry), and the exact conditions under which the pen tester's finding *is* valid.\n\n10. **Legacy → Stripe migration strategy** — Mermaid flowchart of the four phases, cohort-based staged rollout table with rollback triggers, Playwright/Cypress test for PAN detection in request bodies, WAF rule as safety net, and the compliance validation checklist (data flow diagram, log sanitization, SAQ A attestation, targeted pen test)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7d294b3efa223f3e (use SendMessage with to: 'a7d294b3efa223f3e' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 46557\ntool_uses: 5\nduration_ms: 269716</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"18a147c2-8db9-4e3b-9844-4822e355c49a","uuid":"97e0ad44-abd4-4cc7-b345-4a1c940bdcca","timestamp":"2026-06-20T18:43:38.823Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Write a comprehensive answer file for the \"Payment Security & PCI Compliance\" section of a payments system frontend system design study guide.\n\nOutput file path: `/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/payments-system/answers/02-payment-security-pci-compliance.md`\n\n## Format rules\n- Title: `# 02. Payment Security & PCI Compliance`\n- Brief section intro paragraph\n- For each question: use `## Q: <question text>` as the heading, then the three-part answer:\n  1. **Problem framing** — restate the question, why it matters\n  2. **Approach** — your solution with specifics, code snippets or Mermaid diagrams where helpful\n  3. **Tradeoffs** — alternatives considered, why this path was chosen\n- Mermaid diagrams: use ```mermaid fenced blocks inline. Keep them focused on data flow, not decoration.\n- Match the depth/length of reference file style (see reference below for tone)\n- Do NOT add a table of contents or summary section\n\n## Questions to answer (all 10 — 7 Core + 3 Deep)\n\n### Core questions:\n1. What is PCI DSS scope, and how does using a hosted payment field solution like Stripe Elements or Braintree's Hosted Fields reduce the scope of your application's PCI compliance obligations?\n2. How does Stripe Elements prevent your page from ever touching raw card data? Walk through the technical mechanism — what is rendered in your DOM, what lives in the Stripe iframe's origin, and what is exchanged between the two contexts via `postMessage`.\n3. Why can't you simply style a cross-origin iframe with CSS from the parent page, and how do payment providers like Stripe solve this problem while keeping card data isolated? What are the trade-offs of their \"appearance\" configuration APIs?\n4. Explain how a Content Security Policy (CSP) should be configured for a checkout page that loads Stripe.js. Which directives are required, which are commonly misconfigured (and why), and what does Stripe itself require you to allow that might feel uncomfortable from a security standpoint?\n5. What is tokenization in the context of card payments? Walk through the lifecycle: the user enters a card number, Stripe creates a `PaymentMethod` object — what is sent to your server, what is never sent, and how does your server use that token to charge the card?\n6. What is the risk of storing a Stripe `PaymentIntent` client secret in `localStorage` vs. a session cookie vs. an in-memory variable, and what happens if a malicious script on your page reads the client secret?\n7. How do you prevent clickjacking on a checkout page, and why is `X-Frame-Options: DENY` insufficient on its own for a page that also embeds legitimate third-party iframes (like Stripe Elements)?\n\n### Deep questions:\n8. Stripe Elements renders card input fields inside cross-origin iframes. Your designer wants pixel-perfect custom fonts loaded from your CDN inside those fields. Walk through exactly what the browser's cross-origin isolation rules allow, what Stripe's `fonts` configuration option does under the hood, and what you cannot achieve regardless of configuration — then propose a design compromise that satisfies both security and branding requirements.\n9. A penetration tester flags that your checkout page makes a `fetch` to `api.yoursite.com/payment-intent` from the browser, and the response contains the PaymentIntent `client_secret`. They argue this is a security vulnerability. How do you evaluate that claim? Under what conditions is it acceptable to expose a client secret to the browser, and what server-side and frontend controls must be in place to make this safe?\n10. You are migrating from a legacy checkout form that directly POSTed raw card data to your own server (PCI SAQ D scope) to a Stripe Elements integration (PCI SAQ A scope). Describe the full migration strategy: how do you run both implementations in parallel for a staged rollout, how do you ensure no raw card data ever hits your new infrastructure, and how do you validate the cutover is complete from a compliance standpoint?\n\n## Reference style\nLook at this existing answer file for tone and structure reference:\n`/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/payments-system/answers/01-checkout-flow-form-ux.md`\n\nAlso look at the Claude reference section files for formatting:\n`/Users/sarthakagarwal/Desktop/projects/prep/system-design/claude/answers/`\n\n## Depth expectations\n- Senior/staff engineer level answers\n- Be concrete: name actual APIs, browser mechanisms, Stripe-specific behavior\n- Use Mermaid flow diagrams for: tokenization lifecycle (Q5), the postMessage mechanism (Q2), the migration rollout (Q10)\n- Cover edge cases and security nuances that distinguish a great answer from a good one\n- Length per question: 300–600 words of prose + optional diagram\n\nWrite the file to the output path specified above.","agentId":"a7d294b3efa223f3e","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 10 questions are present, the file is 599 lines. Done.\n\nHere's a summary of what was written to `/answers/02-payment-security-pci-compliance.md`:\n\n---\n\n**What's in the file (599 lines, 10 questions answered):**\n\n**Core Questions:**\n\n1. **PCI DSS scope & SAQ reduction** — Explains SAQ A vs. A-EP vs. D tiers with a comparison table, and exactly why hosted fields qualify you for SAQ A (no PAN touches your origin).\n\n2. **Stripe Elements postMessage mechanism** — Mermaid sequence diagram tracing the full lifecycle from `elements.create()` → iframe injection → user typing → SDK `postMessage` → direct iframe-to-Stripe API submission → `pm_...` token returned to your page. Covers what `onChange` events contain (metadata only, never PAN).\n\n3. **Cross-origin iframe styling** — Explains why parent CSS is blocked by same-origin policy, how Stripe's `appearance` config API serializes styles and injects them via `postMessage` into the iframe's own document, and which CSS properties are achievable vs. impossible regardless of configuration.\n\n4. **CSP configuration for Stripe** — Full production CSP header with directive-by-directive breakdown, covers the `frame-src`, `connect-src` (for `q.stripe.com` Radar pixels), and the uncomfortable `script-src https://js.stripe.com` trust decision. Flags `unsafe-inline` and missing `report-uri` as the two most common misconfigurations.\n\n5. **Tokenization lifecycle** — Mermaid sequence diagram covering browser → Stripe API (iframe-direct) → your server → Stripe API (secret key) → webhook. Table showing exactly what goes where (PAN: never to your server; `pm_...`: safe to log).\n\n6. **Client secret storage risks** — Ranked from `localStorage` (worst) to in-memory React state (correct), with XSS attack code example showing how a stored secret is exfiltrated, plus the bounded-capability analysis of what an attacker can actually *do* with a stolen client secret.\n\n7. **Clickjacking defense** — Why `X-Frame-Options: DENY` is not broken alongside Stripe iframes (the header governs who frames *you*, not who *you* frame), why CSP `frame-ancestors` supersedes XFO, and when JavaScript frame-busting is appropriate as defense-in-depth.\n\n**Deep Questions:**\n\n8. **Custom fonts in cross-origin iframes** — Explains that `@font-face` in parent CSS is inaccessible from the iframe, walks through how Stripe's `fonts` array triggers the iframe to construct its own `@font-face` rule and fetch from your CDN (requiring CORS headers), and lists what's architecturally impossible (variable font axes, per-field fonts). Proposes the practical design compromise.\n\n9. **Pen tester's client secret finding** — Structured evaluation framework: what the client secret *can and cannot* do, the five server-side controls required to bound its blast radius (auth, server-determined amount, userId metadata binding, webhook validation, short expiry), and the exact conditions under which the pen tester's finding *is* valid.\n\n10. **Legacy → Stripe migration strategy** — Mermaid flowchart of the four phases, cohort-based staged rollout table with rollback triggers, Playwright/Cypress test for PAN detection in request bodies, WAF rule as safety net, and the compliance validation checklist (data flow diagram, log sanitization, SAQ A attestation, targeted pen test)."}],"resolvedModel":"blackboxai/anthropic/claude-sonnet-4.5","totalDurationMs":269716,"totalTokens":46557,"totalToolUseCount":5,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":878,"cache_read_input_tokens":44840,"output_tokens":838,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":878},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":2,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":2,"editFileCount":1,"linesAdded":600,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
